    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Medical therapy to control seizures and",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "reduce CSF production.",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If signs are progressive, surgical cyst fenestration through craniotomy or craniectomy can result in clinical improvement. When hydrocephalus is present, surgical shunting can be considered. Other disease process must be ruled out before considering surgery. The cystic structure may",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "be an incidental finding.",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Stable patients can be discharged with recommended medical",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "therapy.",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Sequential assessment of pupillary size and reaction to light, and mentation are helpful to determine risk of brain herniation.",
          "pdf_page": 1259,
          "printed_page": 1177,
          "column": 2
        }
      ],
